#
# Adaptation: await existing CM native results; only full owner snapshots/events
# update authorization, permissions, and closed state. CM events trigger a full
# refresh because event/Future ports can deliver out of order. Keep click/mouse
# guards fail-closed and avoid CM credential reads. Intercept native CM close
# before startup; close only after a current full snapshot has no live peers.
# Await checked host Stop only at final main-process exit, not homepage hide,
# and serialize main-window close across checked stop and failure recovery.
# Retain native initialization/host errors until the global listener is ready.
# Surface them through the existing home error card and CM error state; keep
# status reads fallible and make initial CM attach/config failure visible.
#
# Paired FRB route-intent contract: hidden boolean rustdeskIdOnly is not proof
# of a user decision. Serialized peer JSON always restores ID-only, including
# a previously saved false. Malformed metadata is rejected. Only a current
# text edit or trusted in-memory/window session handoff can retain manual
# intent; LAN selections and derived copies never silently choose direct.
# Peer IDs, text, titles, cache keys and window IDs remain raw. At the three
# FFI session routing boundaries only, encode ID-only intent as exactly
# rd-engine-id-only-v1:// + base64url_no_pad(UTF-8 raw ID). This is a restriction,
# never an authentication or input-permission grant. Window reuse/handoff and
# cached session data must preserve the (raw ID, intent) pair.
# This consumer MUST be paired with the matching FRB decoder/session fence.
# Web cannot consume this FRB marker and explicitly rejects ID-only startup.
# Unsupported file/port-forward/terminal modes keep their existing refusal;
# their callers remain conservatively ID-only, not newly implemented modes.
#
# Account safety and excluded-feature patches remain prerequisites, unmodified.
# No new native API, generated code, dependency, or build-script change.
# Validation is patch/text replay only; no formatter, linter, build, tests,
# dependency resolution, application launch, or visual/runtime acceptance.
#
